.TH auto-change-uid 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-change-uid - Change numeric UID of a user

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-change-uid [--silent] username new-uid [directory ...]
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
--silent    Option to proceed without progress output
username    Name of the user whose UID is to change
new-uid     New numeric UID of the user
directory   Optional directories in which to update user ownership
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-change-uid
command changes the numeric UID (user ID) of user "username".

If additional arguments are present following the username and
new UID, they are taken as directories and searched
for files and directories owned by "username", updating the
ownership of all that match.

If --silent is specified, messages indicating the progress of
the change are suppressed.

Note: No file ownerships are updated outside the directories
explicitly indicated.  This allows ownership correction, which
is time-consuming, to be done separately if desired.

Note: Although the underlying DragonFly|FreeBSD pw(8) command
accepts either user name or UID for UID changes, we currently accept
only username in order to simplify the Linux usermod(8) interface.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-change-uid --silent joeuser 2001 /home/joeuser
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/passwd
.ad
.fi

